\begin{abstract}
Scene-text recognition (STR) is effectively solved for Latin script yet fails for most of the
world's writing systems, and collecting labeled real images for every script is not a scalable
answer. We ask whether a recognizer can read a script for which \emph{no real labeled image exists
at all}. We construct a shared grapheme pivot space spanning nine Brahmic scripts and fine-tune a
compact open vision--language model in it under a leave-one-script-out protocol: the held-out
script is seen only as rendered synthetic images, never as a real photograph. On real scene-text
benchmarks the held-out scripts reach \wrrBmin--\wrrBmax\% word recognition (character accuracy up
to \chaBbengali\%), from source-only baselines of \wrrAtamil--\wrrAdevanagari\%. To put that on a
practical rather than an abstract scale, we score stock per-script Tesseract --- which \emph{was}
trained on real labeled images of each target script --- on the identical crops under the identical
metric: our zero-real-image model beats it on \tessScriptsWon\ of nine (\wrrBmean\% vs.\
\tessMean\% macro-average). A prompted frontier VLM (Qwen2.5-VL-7B), scored with every generosity
--- its prose answers parsed in its favor --- reads only Bengali and Devanagari with any competence
(\vlmWrrBengali/\vlmWrrDevanagari\% WRR) and loses on \vlmScriptsWon\ of nine, so scale alone does
not close the gap. Replacing the grapheme pivot with the stock BPE tokenizer under identical data,
seed and recipe degrades word recognition on all nine scripts
($\bpeRatioMin\times$--$\bpeRatioMax\times$; exact sign test $p=\bpeSignP$), on three of them
\emph{despite equal-or-better character accuracy} --- isolating output-space structure, not
synthetic fine-tuning per se, as what converts character evidence into whole-word reads.

The campaign ran end-to-end under a frozen preregistration, with every quantitative forecast
committed and pushed before the run it predicted, and we report that record whole --- it is mostly
a record of misses. The preregistered primary, that tokenizer fertility predicts which scripts
transfer, was \emph{falsified} (Spearman $\spearFert$). Synthetic exposure yields a clean
log-linear dose--response with a script-invariant slope ($\scaleSlope$ WRR per doubling, $R^2$ up
to \scaleRtwoMax), but the pre-committed slope \emph{magnitude} missed by $\scaleSlopeRatio\times$;
pivot coverage orders scripts in-sample yet does not generalize out-of-sample ($r=\covOOSr$); and
an out-of-benchmark probe on Khmer, forecast at \khmerPred\% before any Khmer recognizer existed,
returned \khmerB\% --- a $\khmerResidRMSE\sigma$ miss that also marks where the method stops. We
therefore claim no predictive law. What we offer is a demonstration, a controlled attribution of
its mechanism, and a forecasting record scored in public against rules fixed in advance --- the
failures included. We release the pivot construction, rendering and verification pipeline, the
zero-real-image evaluation protocol, and the preregistration chain.
\end{abstract}
